  "historical_output_text": " \n\nOne common weather pattern is a cold front. When a cold air mass moves into an area occupied by warmer air, it creates a cold front. The cold air is denser and heavier than the warm air, so it pushes under the warm air and displaces it. \n\nAs the cold air moves in, it lifts the warm, less dense air, causing it to cool and condense into clouds and precipitation. This is why cold fronts often bring storms with heavy rain, thunderstorms, and strong winds. \n\nThe cold air also tends to be drier than the warm air it replaces, so after the front passes, the area may become cooler and drier. Cold fronts can move quickly, sometimes covering hundreds of miles in a day. They are a major factor in determining the day-to-day weather in many parts of the world.\n\nSo in",
